Database Setup (Render)
Create Postgres Instance: Log in to Render, click New +, and select PostgreSQL. Name your database and select the Free Tier.
Local Connection: Once the database is Available, find the External Database URL.
Run Scripts: Use your preferred SQL tool, or psql in the terminal, to connect to the Render instance using that External URL. Run your create_db.sql and fill_db.sql scripts to build the schema and seed data.

Prepare Settings.py
Before pushing your code to GitHub, you must ensure Django is configured for the cloud environment.
Comment Out Local Database: In backend/medtracker/settings.py, comment out your local SQLite or local Postgres configuration.
Uncomment Production Database: Ensure the code using dj_database_url is active. You will need to import dj_database_url and os at the top. Set the DATABASES default to dj_database_url.config using the DATABASE_URL environment variable and set conn_max_age to 0.
Internal Pathing: Ensure ALLOWED_HOSTS includes your Render domain, such as https://www.google.com/search?q=csce524project.onrender.com, as well as localhost.

Web Service Configuration (Render)
Create Web Service: Click New +, select Web Service, and connect your GitHub repository.
Environment Variables: Go to the Environment tab. Add a variable named DATABASE_URL. Paste the Internal Database URL from your Render Postgres dashboard, not the External one.
Start Command: Under Settings, update the Start Command to: gunicorn --chdir backend --bind 0.0.0.0:$PORT medtracker.wsgi

Update Client Application
To test the live deployment, you must point your local console app to the cloud.
Change URL: Open service_console_app.py.
Update Base URL: Replace http://localhost:8000/api with your live Render URL, for example: https://csce524project.onrender.com/api/
Verify: Run the app locally. It should now fetch data from the Render Postgres instance via the Render Web Service.